1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,359
War story from the bike-share team,
Tuesday. Diff adds one line to the Gradle
build: the PostgreSQL JDBC driver. No Java
changes. Restart. Startup dies: Failed to
configure a DataSource: 'url' attribute is
not specified.

2
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:31,240
Nobody wrote a DataSource @Bean. Something
else did — Boot auto-configuration —
because a DataSource class became visible
and no user bean of that type existed yet.
The classpath spoke; Boot answered.

3
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:49,920
Auto-configuration is conditional bean
registration shipped with Boot. Candidate
classes are listed under META-INF/spring/o
rg.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.Auto
Configuration.imports (older Boot:
spring.factories).

4
00:00:50,039 --> 00:01:04,599
Annotations like @ConditionalOnClass,
@ConditionalOnMissingBean,
@ConditionalOnProperty decide whether
their @Bean methods fire. Your application
code stays small; the classpath and
Environment become signals.

5
00:01:04,719 --> 00:01:13,480
That is powerful and surprising in equal
measure until you can read a condition
evaluation report. Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this example.

6
00:01:13,599 --> 00:01:18,799
# auto-configured DataSource still needs
properties Walk the simplified auto-
config.

7
00:01:18,919 --> 00:01:31,879
@ConditionalOnClass means: only consider
this configuration if DataSource and
Hikari are loadable — adding the Postgres
driver pulled those types onto the
classpath (together with Boot's JDBC
starter or transitive deps).

8
00:01:31,999 --> 00:01:39,639
@EnableConfigurationProperties(DataSourceP
roperties.class) binds spring.datasource.*
into a typed properties object.

9
00:01:39,759 --> 00:01:47,639
@Bean + @ConditionalOnMissingBean means:
create a DataSource *only if* the user did
not already define one.

10
00:01:47,759 --> 00:02:00,039
initializeDataSourceBuilder().build()
reads URL, username, password from those
properties — empty URL is what triggered
Tuesday's failure. Runtime after the
driver lands, step by step.

11
00:02:00,159 --> 00:02:10,879
During context refresh Boot loads auto-
configuration import lists. Each candidate
is evaluated against conditions.
DataSourceAutoConfiguration matches on
class presence.

12
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:22,520
Inside, @ConditionalOnMissingBean searches
the bean factory for an existing
DataSource definition — none in the bike-
share app — so the method is eligible. It
builds from DataSourceProperties.

13
00:02:22,639 --> 00:02:34,679
Properties are empty for url → Boot fails
fast with the familiar message. That fail-
fast is kinder than a silent miswire to an
in-memory default you did not want.

14
00:02:34,799 --> 00:02:44,879
Fixes: add spring.datasource.url (and
credentials); or define your own @Bean
DataSource so @ConditionalOnMissingBean
skips;

15
00:02:44,999 --> 00:02:57,799
or exclude the auto-config class via
spring.autoconfigure.exclude /
@SpringBootApplication(exclude=...) if you
truly have no database yet but need the
driver for a batch tool on the same
classpath.

16
00:02:57,919 --> 00:03:03,719
Debug with --debug or
ConditionEvaluationReport logging to see
matched and negative conditions.

17
00:03:03,839 --> 00:03:16,279
Failure mode symptoms beyond missing URL:
you define a custom DataSource bean, but
still see Boot trying to configure JPA
against a second implicit pool — often
because another auto-config keyed off
different types,

18
00:03:16,399 --> 00:03:24,239
or your bean was not visible yet when
conditions ran (rare with proper user
config ordering, more common with unusual
BeanDefinition tricks).

19
00:03:24,359 --> 00:03:33,039
Symptom of successful override: condition
report shows DataSourceAutoConfiguration
beans skipped due to
@ConditionalOnMissingBean.

20
00:03:33,159 --> 00:03:44,239
Symptom of accidental activation: adding
starter-data-jpa "for later" pulls driver
+ Hibernate and suddenly entity scanning
and DDL expectations appear. Trade-offs.

21
00:03:44,359 --> 00:03:56,239
Auto-configuration collapses days of
XML/Java infra into classpath conventions
and keeps services consistent; it couples
startup behavior to dependencies, so "just
add a jar" is never free.

22
00:03:56,359 --> 00:04:07,799
Exclusions and custom beans restore
control but require knowing which auto-
config class owns the surprise. Prefer
reading conditions over memorizing every
@Bean Boot might create.

23
00:04:07,919 --> 00:04:16,199
One more runtime detail the bike-share
team used after Tuesday: start with
--debug and search the log for
DataSourceAutoConfiguration.

24
00:04:16,319 --> 00:04:25,079
Positive matches show which nested
configurations fired; negative matches
show why an exclude or
@ConditionalOnMissingBean skipped a bean.

25
00:04:25,199 --> 00:04:35,719
That report is how you prove "our @Bean
DataSource suppressed Boot" without
guessing. Auto-configuration is not a
black box once you treat conditions as the
API.

26
00:04:35,839 --> 00:04:49,079
Misconception unique to auto-
configuration: "Auto-configuration runs
before my @Configuration and always wins."
Conditions like @ConditionalOnMissingBean
are evaluated with user beans in mind;

27
00:04:49,199 --> 00:04:52,519
defining your own DataSource typically
suppresses Boot's.

28
00:04:52,639 --> 00:05:04,119
The surprise is not that Boot overrides
you — it is that Boot acts when you said
nothing and the classpath said "database."
The team now understands why the driver
created a bean.

29
00:05:04,239 --> 00:05:17,199
The new hire still stares at a pom.xml
with twenty-seven versioned Spring jars
fighting each other. Curated dependency
sets — starters — are how Boot teams avoid
that hell on purpose.
